          [ [1m-D [4m[22mrgmdir[24m ] [ [1m-m [4m[22mmissing_sec[24m ] [ [1m-r [4m[22mrecheck_sec[24m ]
          [ [1m-R [4m[22mrestart_sec[24m ] [ [1m-u [4m[22muser[24m ] [ [1m-z [4m[22mloop_sec[24m ] [ [4munstuck.log[24m ]

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
     [4mRog-O-Matic[24m (hereafter referred to as [1mrogomatic[22m) is a computer fantasy game
     with a newer twist.  It is CRT (Cathode Rat Tube) oriented and  the  object
     of the game is to automatically survive the attacks of various monsters and
     get  a  lot  of gold, rather than having to actually type the required com-
     mands yourself.

     The [1mrogomatic [22mplays the game of [1mrogue[22m(6) on your behalf while you watch.

     Once you have entered the world of [1mrogomatic [22myou will stay there, passively
     watching the game unfold before you much like a catatonic teenager watching
     television.  Should you wish to intervene, type the [1mt [22mcommand and [1mrogomatic[0m
     will enter transparent mode.  See the [1mCOMMANDS [22msection below for details on
     commands, and on transparent mode.

     When [1mrogomatic [22mlaunches [1mrogue[22m(6)  via  the  [1mplayer  [22mprogram,  it  sets  the
     [1mROGUEOPTS  [22menvironment  variable  to  a value such that the rogue name used
     will start with [1mrogo-[22m.  The rogue score path to  [1m@RGMDIR@/rogue.scr[22m,  which
     is [1mNOT [22mthe default rogue score file.  Thus by default, [1mrogomatic [22mgame stats
     will  not  compete  with the humans who are playing [1mrogue[22m(6) in the default
     way.

[1mOPTIONS[0m
     [1m-h     [22mPrint the usage message, and exit with a status of [1m2[22m.

     [1m-V     [22mPrint the [1mrogomatic [22mversion string and exit with a status of [1m2[22m.

     [1m-a [4m[22msecs[0m
            Set the timeout timer to [1msecs [22mseconds, and set the  [4mROGOTIMER[24m  envi-
            ronment variable to [1msecs[22m.

            The  [1msecs  [22mmust  be  a  value >= 0, and it may an integer, or a real
            value (i.e., 4.5).  By default, [1msecs [22mis set to [1m0.0[22m,  which  has  the
            effect of not setting a timeout timer.

            For example, using [1m-a [4m[22m4.5[24m on the command line will cause the program
            attempt to resume normal operation after a hang while reading a mes-
            sage from [1mrogue[22m(6).

            Before  [1mrogomatic[22m(6) attempts to read from [1mrogue[22m(6), a timeout alarm
            will be set.  If the read  hangs  for  more  than  [4msecs[24m  seconds,  a
            [1mSIGARLM [22msignal will be delivered to the player program.  The [1mSIGARLM[0m
            is  then  caught and a [1msiglongjmp[22m(3) performed a non-local jump to a
            control point where player program is much  more  likely  to  resume
            normal  operation.  Nevertheless, there are circumstances where non-
            local jump still hangs.

            See the section on [1mBUGS [22mbelow for more information on hanging.

     [1m-c     [22mTurn on "[4mcreative[24m [4mmode[24m".

            In "[4mcreative[24m [4mmode[24m", a number of strategies  are  employed,  many  of
            which  are used by wide variety of human players, such as using trap
            arrows.

            Unfortunately a number of these  "[4mcreative[24m  [4mmode[24m  strategies  mostly
            work on very old version of [1mrogue[22m(6) that are no longer supported.

            Historically, this mode was called "cheat mode" even though the same
            strategies were used by wide variety of human players.

            When  using  with  [1mrogue[22m(6)  with  [1mrogue version 5.4.5 [22mor later, the
            "[4mcreative[24m [4mmode[24m" is enabled by default.

            See also the [1mc [22mcommand in the in transparent mode  in  the  [1mCOMMANDS[0m
            section below.

     [1m-d     [22mUse a UTC date and time sub-directory under rogomatic directory path
            to store files.

            By default, [1mrogomatic [22mcreates the rogomatic directory:

                 [4m@RGMDIR@[0m

            With the [1m-d [22moption, [1mrogomatic [22mcreates a new rogomatic sub-directory,
            using a UTC date and time, of the form:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS[0m

            where [4mYYYYMMDD_HHMMSS[24m is the year, month, day, hour, minute and sec-
            ond, in the UTC timezone, of when player program started.

            Because  [1m-d  [22mcreates  a new rogomatic sub-directory, the environment
            starts out in an initial state.  This will make is easier to  use  a
            debugger  such as [1mlldb[22m(1), especially when combined with [1m-S [4m[22mROGOSEED[0m
            making the action much more deterministic from  a  debugging  stand-
            point.   See  the [1mDEBUGGING [22msection near the bottom of this man page
            for more details.

     [1m-D [4m[22mrgmdir[0m
            Change the rogomatic directory path to [4mrgmdir[24m.

            The use of [1m-d [22mcommand line option combined with [1m-D [4m[22mrgmdir[24m will  case
            [1mrogomatic [22mto create and use files under:

                 [4mrgmdir[24m/YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS

     [1m-e     [22mTurn [1mOFF [22mrogomatic game logging.

            By  default, rogomatic game logging is enabled, and the [4mgamelog[24m file
            is written to:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/gamelog[0m

            [1mNOTE[22m: Prior to version 14.2.6, this command line  option  turned  on
            rogomatic  game logging.  Now that rogomatic game logging is enabled
            by default, and the [1m-e [22mdisables rogomatic game logging.

            See also the [1me [22mcommand in transparent mode.

     [1m-E     [22mStart up in emacs mode.

     [1m-f [4m[22mrogue[0m
            Set the path of [1mrogue[22m, overriding the default.

            By default, [1mrogomatic [22mfirst looks for:

                 [1m./rogue[0m

            in the current directory, then it looks for:

                 [1m../rogue5.4/rogue[0m

            before looking for [1mrogue (6) [22min the  default  installation  location
            of: [1m@BINDIR@/rogue[22m.

            [1mIMPORTANT NOTE[22m:  The  [1m$PATH [22menvironment variable is [1mNOT [22mused to find
            [1mrogue (6).  [22mTherefore, the [4mrogue[24m argument needs to be a path.

            The [1mrogomatic[22m(6) command might be able to  play with  versions  3.6,
            5.2, 5.3, 5.4, 5.4.4, and 5.4.5.

            You  can  run [1mrogomatic [22mon modified rogues using this option, but be
            warned that the interface must be completely compatible with one  of
            the  standard  versions  of  [1mrogue  [22mlisted,  and  the version number
            printed must be either "3.6," "5.2," "5.3", "5.4", or "5.4.5".

            [1mIMPORTANT NOTE[22m: Only [1mrogue version 5.4.5  [22mas  found  in  the  GitHub
            repo:

                 [4mhttps://github.com/lcn2/rogue5.4[0m

            has been tested with this version of [1mrogomatic[22m(6).  While other ver-
            sions  of  rogue  may  work, only [1mrogue version 5.4.5 [22mwith a minimum
            date of [1m2026-08-03 [22mis supported.

     [1m-G [4m[22mgoodlvl[0m
            Set the "[1mgood game[22m" level to [4mgoodlvl[24m.

            A "[1mgood game[22m" is when the rogue reaches a dungeon level of at  least
            [4mgoodlvl[24m.   When  a  rogue has the [4mAmulet[24m [4mof[24m [4mYendor[24m at the end of the
            game, then the game is also considered a "[1mgood game[22m".

            By default, [4mgoodlvl[24m level is [1m@GOODGAME@[22m.

            When a "[1mgood game[22m" exits, the rogomatic game log file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/gamelog[0m

            is preserved by renaming it to a file of the form:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/goodgame.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS.max_level.score.mnst[0m

            See the [1mFILES [22msection below for details on the goodname file form.

     [1m-H     [22mDisable the so-called rogomatic "[4mhalftime[24m [4mshow[24m".

            When [1mrogomatic [22mdetects that [1mrogue [22mhas reached level 2,  it  plays  a
            short character based "movie" on the screen.

            The use of [1m-t [22mor [1m-E [22mimplies [1m-H[22m.

            Historically,  this  option was [1m-h[22m, however recently that option be-
            came the "help option", and so it was named as [1m-H[22m.

     [1m-p     [22mReplay the rogomatic game log file.

            The default rogomatic game log file is:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/gamelog[0m

            When combined with a final [4mr_file[24m command line argument, the  replay
            file will be [4mr_file[24m.

     [1m-P [4m[22mplayer[0m
            Set the path to the player program.

     [1m-q     [22mSet [1mquiet mode[22m, and disable [1mncurses[22m(3X) output.

            By  default,  output  from  rogue[1m(6), [22malong with additional messages
            from the [1mplayer [22mprocess are written to standard output so that a hu-
            man may watch the game as it is played.  The use of [1m-q [22mwill  silence
            nearly all output, allowing in theory, the game to be played faster.

            When quite mode, all file logging continues as normal.  One may wish
            to  tail  a  log file such as [1mtotal.lvllog[22m, or even the more verbose
            [1mgamelog [22mto track progress.  Such files are under the  rogomatic  di-
            rectory.  When using the [1mtail[22m(1) utility, one would use both the [1m-f[22m,
            and the [1m-F [22mcommand line options:

                 [1mtail -f -F [4m[22mRGMDIR[24m[1m/total.lvllog[0m

            where  [4mRGMDIR[24m  is  the  rogomatic  directory  (such  as  [1m@RGMDIR@ [22mor
            [1m/var/tmp/rogo [22mor [1m/var/tmp/mrogo/rogo.[4m[22mnumber[24m).

            The use of [1m-q [22moverrides and disables any use of user mode  (disables
            [1m-u[22m), and use of watched mode (disables [1m-w[22m).

            See the [1mFILES [22msection for more details on log files.

     [1m-r     [22mRestore a saved rogue game.

            Without  a  final  [4mr_file[24m  command  line  option, run [1mrogue [22mwith the
            [1m-r [22m@RGMDIR@/rogue.sav command line option and restore the rogue game
            stored in that file.

            If a final [4mr_file[24m command line argument is given, run [1mrogue[22m(6)  will
            be  run  with  [1m-r [4m[22mr_file[24m  so  that  rogue game will be restored from
            [4mr_file[24m.

     [1m-S [4m[22mROGOSEED[0m
            Seed  the  pseudo-random  number  generator  to  [4mROGOSEED[24m  for  both
            [1mrogue[22m(6) and rogomatic.

            When  combined with [1m-d [22mthis creates much more deterministic run from
            a debugging standpoint.

     [1m-t     [22mTerse mode.  In terse mode, [1mrogomatic [22mwill only print a summary  af-
            ter each level explored.

     [1m-u     [22mStart  up  in user mode.  This tells [1mrogomatic [22mto start up in trans-
            parent mode, so the user can type commands before  [1mrogomatic  [22mstarts
            exploring level 1.

            The use of [1m-q [22mdisables user mode, and overrides this flag.

     [1m-U [4m[22musec[0m
            Set the sleep time between actions to [4musec[24m microseconds.

            Between  writes  to  [1mrogue[22m(6) the player program will sleep [4musec[24m mi-
            croseconds.  This has the effect of slowing down the actions on  the
            screen to be somewhat viewable by humans.

            To have no such delays, use [1m-H -U 0 [22mon the command line.

            By default, [4musec[24m is set to [1m@USLEEP@ [22mmicroseconds.

     [1m-w     [22mStart up in watched mode.

            The use of [1m-q [22mdisables watch mode, and overrides this flag.

     [1m-s  [ [4m[22mrogue_ver[24m [1m][0m
            Print  the  [1mrogomatic  scoreboard[22m, reverse sorted by the ending gold
            amount, of the rogomatic game results.

            The [1mrogomatic scoreboard [22mis printed with the following fields:


                   1. Year
                        End of game 4-digit year (YYYY), in the local timezone

                   2. Month
                        End of game 3-letter month (Mon), in the local timezone

                   3. Day
                        End of game 2-digit day (01-31), in the local timezone

                   4. Time
                        End of game time of day (24-hour HH:MM:SS format, in the
                        local timezone

                   5. User
                        rogue username that will start with rogo-.

                   6. Gold
                        End of game gold that will include the 10% penalty  when
                        killed, or payment for inventory items if a total winner

                   7. Killed by
                        Cause of death, or "total winner

                   8. Level
                        The deepest dungeon level reached

                   9. HpEnd of game points

                   10. Str
                        End of game strength

                   11. Ac
                        End of game armor class that was being worn

                   12. Level/Exp
                        End  of  game  experience level followed by / experience
                        points

                   13. Game number
                        The logged game number, starting with 0

                   14. Dungeon
                        The rogue dungeon number

            The default [1mrogue_ver [22mis [1m5.4.5[22m.

            The default rogomatic scoreboard is:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/rgmscore[4m[22m5.4.5[0m

            If [1m-s [4m[22mrogue_ver[24m is given, then the rogomatic scoreboard printed will
            be from:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/rgmscore[4m[22mrogue_ver[0m

            where [1mrogue_ver [22mis the [1mrogue [22mversion number, such as [4m5.4.5[24m.

            If a rogomatic delta file is found:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/rgmdelta[4m[22mrogue_ver[0m

            The rogomatic delta is merged into  the  rogomatic  scoreboard,  and
            then the rogomatic delta file is removed.

            If just the [1m-s [22mcommand line option is used, then the [1m-s [22mcommand line
            option  [1mMUST BE THE LAST OPTION ON THE COMMAND LINE[22m,  and  cannot be
            used in conjunction with a final [4mr_file[24m.

     [ [4mr_file[24m ]
            When used with [1m-p[22m, [4mr_file[24m specifies the  rogomatic  game  to  replay
            from.

            When used with [1m-r[22m, [4mr_file[24m specifies the rogue game to restore from.

            Use of [4mr_file[24m is [1mNOT [22mcompatible with using [1m-s [22mby itself.

[1mUSEFUL UTILITIES[0m
     Several useful utilities are installed under the [1m@BINDIR@ [22mdirectory, or may
     be run from top of the source code directory.

     [1mrun_rogo[0m
            The  [1mrun_rogo  [22mcommand  is a very useful way to run [1mrogomatic[22m(6) and
            set the rogomatic directory to:

                 [1m/var/tmp/rogo[0m

            instead of the usual default rogomatic directory.  By  changing  the
            rogomatic  directory  to  a  non-default value, manual runs of [1mrogo-[0m
            [1mmatic[22m(6) will not interfere (such a lock file contention).

            In addition, the [1mrun_rogo [22mcommand will first look in the  local  di-
            rectory for commands, and use them if found, otherwise it search and
            use commands along the [1m$PATH [22menvironment variable path.

            See [1mrun_rogo -h [22mfor usage details.

     [1mrerun_rogo[0m
            The [1mrerun_rogo [22mcommand is a very useful to run [1mrogomatic[22m(6) multiple
            times  in a row.  Currently, when the rogue dies in the dungeon, [1mro-[0m
            [1mgomatic[22m(6) exits.  When run with [1mrerun_rogo [22ma new [1mrogomatic[22m(6)  will
            be started after the previous [1mrogomatic[22m(6) exits.

            The  [1mrerun_rogo  [22muses  the [1mrun_rogo [22mcommand to run [1mrogomatic[22m(6).  As
            such, the rogomatic directory will be set to:

                 [1m/var/tmp/rogo[0m

            If the file [1m.stopfile [22mis created in the  rogomatic  directory  while
            [1mrerun_rogo [22mis running, then the next time [1mrogue[22m(6) exits, the [1m.stop-[0m
            [1mfile  [22mfile  is  removed, and rerun cycle will be stopped.  This is a
            graceful way to end the rerun cycle.

            Because  [1mrerun_rogo  [22mchanges  the  default  rogomatic  directory  to
            [1m/var/tmp/rogo[22m,  the default way to top the rerun cycle is to use the
            command:

                 [1mtouch /var/tmp/rogo/.stopfile[0m

            Changing the rogomatic  directory  by  supplying  [1m-D [4m[22mrgmdir[24m  implies
            [1m-s [4m[22mrgmdir/.stopfile[24m as well.

            To  deal  with the possibility of [1mrogomatic[22m(6) hanging, and to speed
            up the automated game play, we recommend [1mNO [22mrelay between writes  to
            [1mrogue[22m(6), as well as a timeout timer:

                 [1mrerun_rogo -H -U 0 -a 4.5[0m

            To  deal  with the possibility of [1mrogomatic[22m(6) hanging, we recommend
            running [1munstuck_player [22min a separate window while running this tool:

                 [1munstuck_player -v 1[0m

            See the section on [1mBUGS [22mbelow for more information on hanging.

            See [1mrerun_rogo -h [22mfor usage details.

     [1mmrerun_rogo[0m
            The [1mmrerun_rogo [22mcommand is a very useful to run  multiple  instances
            of the [1mrerun_rogo [22mcommand.

            The  [1mmrerun_rogo [22mcommand will establish a rogomatic directory of the
            form:

                 [1m/var/tmp/mrogo/rogo.ID[0m

            where [4mID[24m is the argument supplied to the [1mrerun_rogo [22mcommand.

            An    instance    of     the     [1munstuck_player     [22mutility     with
            [1m-D [4m[22m/var/tmp/mrogo/rogo.ID[24m being set.

            Then   the   [1mmrerun_rogo   [22mcommand   will   be  run  with  the  same
            [1m-D [4m[22m/var/tmp/mrogo/rogo.ID[24m being set.

            Any other options, except [1m-O [4m[22mrerun_rogo[24m will be passed down  to  the
            subsequent utilities as needed.

     [1mend_player[0m
            The  [1mend_player  [22mcommand  will  attempt  to  end  a [1mrogomatic[22m(6) and
            [1mrogue[22m(6) run.

            It will first attempt to send a [1mSIGHUP [22msignal to [1mrogue[22m(6) by way  of
            the  [1mkillall[22m(1)  command.   This allows [1mrogue[22m(6) to gracefully exit,
            and potentially record a score in the rogue game file.  The cause of
            death will be reported as "[4mby[24m  [4msignal[24m".   When  the  happens,  [1mrogo-[0m
            [1mmatic[22m(6)  will consider the rogue as having undergone a normal death
            and will update the rogomatic state accordingly.

            See [1mend_player -h [22mfor usage details.

     [1munstuck_player[0m
            The [1munstuck_player [22mcommand attempt to detect if a  [1mrogue[22m(6)  program
            is stalled by observing that the process isn't accumulating CPU time
            by  checking the output of a [1mps[22m(1) command.  The common reason for a
            stalled [1mrogue[22m(6) program is that [1mrogomatic[22m(6) has hung.

            See the section on [1mBUGS [22mbelow for more information on hanging.

            We recommended that a minor level of verbosity  be  used  with  this
            tool, as in:

                 [1munstuck_player -v 1[0m

            When  a [1mrogue[22m(6) program has not accumulated CPU time after a period
            of time (default: after 16 seconds), a [1mSIGHUP [22msignal is set  to  the
            [1mrogue[22m(6) program.

            See [1mend_player [22mabove for what happens when a [1mSIGHUP [22msignal is set to
            the [1mrogue[22m(6) program.

            If  a  [1mSIGHUP  [22msignal cannot be delivered to a [1mrogue[22m(6) program, say
            because no such program is found, then a [1mSIGHUP [22msignal is set to the
            [1mplayer [22mprogram.

            See [1munstuck_player -h [22mfor usage details.

     The following tools are less well documented and less well supported:

     [1mrgmgene[0m
            The [1mrgmgene [22mcommand summarizes the current population  in  the  gene
            pool.

     [1mrgmscatter[0m
            The  [1mrgmscatter  [22mcommand  prints a scatter plot of all scores in the
            scorefile.

     [1mrgmhist[0m
            The [1mrgmhist [22mcommand prints a histogram of score (or levels if the [1m-l[0m
            option is given).

[1mCOMMANDS[0m
     The following commands may be entered from the keyboard while  [1mrogomatic[22m(6)
     is running.

     Some useful keyboard commands are:

     [1mt      [4m[22mToggle,[24m toggles the setting of the [4mtransparent[24m mode flag.  In trans-
            parent  mode, [1mrogomatic [22mwill not automatically attack monsters, pick
            up treasure, or explore around.  Unfortunately, there is no  way  to
            enter  complicated  commands like [4mquaff[24m or [4mread[24m in transparent mode.
            Typing any [1mrogue [22mcommand, or single-step command also enables trans-
            parent mode.

            The [1mt [22mcommand will cause [1mrogomatic [22mwill enter transparent mode.

            In transparent mode, you can type several commands, the most  useful
            of  which  is  the  [1m?  [22mcommand, which prints out the next help line.
            Press the [1m?  [22mkey repeatedly to see all of  the  help.   Some  useful
            commands  are:  [1mh j k l H J K L b n y u f s > < S Q.  [22mThese have the
            same meaning as in normal [1mrogue[22m.  To return to [1mrogomatic [22mmode,  type
            the [1mt [22mcommand again.

     [1mQ      [4m[22mQuit,[24m  tells [1mrogomatic [22mto quit the game.  The score is logged in the
            score file, and possibly the [1mrogue [22mtop ten, and the game  is  termi-
            nated.

     [1mS      [4m[22mSave,[24m  the game is saved to the default save file, set by the [4m<file>[0m
            argument to [4mrogomatic[24m or the [4mROGUEOPTS[24m  environment  variable.   The
            game may be restarted later.

     [1mc      [4m[22mcreative[24m [4mmode[24m,  tells  [1mrogomatic [22mto take advantage of idiosyncrasies
            in the [1mrogue [22mgame to get a higher score.

            Note that this option should only be used on very  old  3.6  version
            [1mrogue[22m, since the VMS version does not exhibit the same bugs.

            The strategies enabled by [4mcreative[24m [4mmode[24m are known and used by a fair
            number of human players.

     [1md      [4m[22mDebugging,[24m  toggles the debugging flag, which is useless to non-wiz-
            ard users (although it is entertaining if your terminal has  reverse
            video).

     [1me      [4m[22mToggle[24m [4mgame[24m [4mlogging.[0m

            By  default rogomatic game logging is enabled (unless the [1m-e [22mcommand
            line option was used).

            When enable, the rogomatic game log file is:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/gamelog[0m

            If the rogomatic game logging was enabled the [1me [22mcommand will disable
            it; otherwise rogomatic game logging will be re-enabled.

            The [1m-p [22moption will replay a saved game.  Additionally, a stop action
            command (carriage return gives a single step) has been added.

     [1mi      [4m[22mInventory,[24m dumps the current inventory on the screen.

     [1mm      [4m[22mMonster[24m [4mlist,[24m shows the current long term memory evaluation  of  the
            monsters.   [1mrogomatic  [22mnows  keeps a long term memory file (for each
            version of [1mrogue[22m) in the score directory, and records  the  meanness
            of each monster it finds.  This command prints out the letter, name,
            and  strength of each monster.  Strength is listed as three numbers:
            expected damage (scaled by 10) and  maximum  damage  (unscaled)  are
            shown in parentheses, and the mean number of arrows required to kill
            a monster is shown in square brackets.

     [1mCR     [4m[22mSingle[24m  [4mstep,[24m a carriage return enables [4mtransparent[24m mode, and causes
            [1mrogomatic [22mto single-step.  The command sent is printed at the bottom
            right hand of the screen.

     [1m/      [4m[22mSnapshot,[24m a timestamp, the current screen, and the current inventory
            are appended to file [4msnapshot.rgm[24m in the current directory.

     [1m$      [4m[22mWeaponrank,[24m dumps [1mrogomatic[22m's current estimate of his weapons  rank-
            ings.

     [1m%      [4m[22mArmorrank,[24m dumps [1mrogomatic[22m's current estimate of his armor rankings.

     [1m=      [4m[22mRingrank,[24m dumps [1mrogomatic[22m's current estimate of his ring rankings.

     [1m-      [4m[22mStatus,[24m  prints  out  a  status  line indicating various things like
            whether [1mrogomatic[22m's is confused, has red hands,  is  wearing  cursed
            armor  or  wielding  a  cursed  weapon, and which monsters have been
            genocided.  It also prints the number of turns elapsed in the game.

     [1m`      [4m[22mSummary,[24m a backquote prints a short summary of the game.

     [1m~      [4m[22mVersion,[24m prints the version of [1mrogue [22mand [1mrogomatic [22mbeing  used.   It
            also prints the current goal score.

     [1mG      [4m[22mGenotype,[24m  displays  the settings of the genetically learned parame-
            ters of the genotype playing now.  See section [4mGenes[24m for more infor-
            mation about these parameters.

     [1mR      [4m[22mReplay[24m [4mposition,[24m allows you to skip around in a log  file  when  re-
            playing  an  old game.  This key must be followed by either [1mf, p, c,[0m
            [1mn, [22mor [1ml.  [22mThese indicate you wish to see the [4mfirst,[24m  [4mprevious,[24m  [4mcur-[0m
            [4mrent,[24m  [4mnext,[24m  or  [1mlast [22mlevels of the game.  The first time you enter
            the [1mR [22mcommand, there will be a pause of several  seconds  while  the
            whole log file is digested.  After this initialization, there should
            be  no  delay  when skipping around in the log file.  After you have
            skipped once, the program won't terminate at the end of the file un-
            til you enter the [1mQ [4m[22m(quit)[24m ommand.  This way you can replay the last
            level over (and over...).

     When the game ends, either by your death, when you quit, or when  [1mrogomatic[0m
     dies  horribly of some bug, you will not get a list of the top-ten scorers.
     The scoring is based entirely upon useless criteria anyway.  [1mrogomatic [22mdoes
     keep a list of its games.  To see the best [1mrogomatic [22mgames, use  "rogomatic
     -s" which dumps the current scores in reverse order.

     For    more   detailed   directions,   as   the   expression   goes:   [1mRTFS[0m
     ([4mhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RTFS[24m) :-).  See:

          [4mhttps://www.github.com/lcn2/rogomatic[0m

[1mGENES[0m
     The [1mG [22mcommand lists the current settings for each [1mrogomatic[22m's ``DNA''  set-
     tings.  These numbers (from 0 to 99) specify some of [1mrogomatic[22m's behaviors.
     A  gene  pool  file is kept in the score directory that records the current
     population of explorers.  A variant of Holland's ``Genetic  Algorithm''  is
     used  to  manipulate  this  gene pool.  The result is that [1mrogomatic [22mlearns
     from his past games how to play better.  The following genes are  currently
     used:

     [1mSr     [22mHow many times to search each square for traps (0 to 4, depending on
            amount of food and how far below level 7 he is).

     [1mDr     [22mHow many times to search each square for secret doors (0 to 19, more
            for dead end passages not in mazes).

     [1mRe     [22mHow  much to rest up when injured (until 40% to 80% healed, based on
            experience level).

     [1mAr     [22mWhen to shoot arrows at enemies, the higher  the  number,  the  more
            likely  [1mrogomatic  [22mis to shoot arrows at monsters (both smaller mon-
            sters, and more arrows).

     [1mEx     [22mHow soon to experiment with unknown  potions  and  scrolls  (between
            levels 1 and 10).

     [1mRn     [22mHow likely he is to run from an opponent (the higher the number, the
            sooner he will run).

     [1mWk     [22mHow likely he is to wake a sleeping opponent (the higher the number,
            the bigger the monster he will attack, even though it is sleeping).

     [1mFd     [22mHow  likely  he is to hoard extra food.  This affects how many rings
            he wears, and how much he searches for traps.

[1mFILES[0m
     There are two executable files: [1mrogomatic [22mand [1mplayer[22m.  The  [1mrogomatic  [22mpro-
     gram  is  a small program which forks and execs the [1mrogue [22mgame connected to
     the [1mplayer [22mprogram.  You must have access to all of these files.

     These programs are searched for in the current directory.  If they are  not
     found  in  the current directory, then, the [1mrogomatic [22mprogram will look for
     then under the [1m@BINDIR@ [22mdirectory.

     By default, [1mrogomatic [22mcreates [1m@RGMDIR@ [22mknown as the rogomatic directory.

     The rogomatic directory may be changed by the command line  option  [1m-D [22mpath
     command line option.

     If the [1m-d [22mcommand line option is used, a new rogomatic directory is created
     and used in sub-directory of the form:

          [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS[0m

     where  [4mYYYYMMDD_HHMMSS[24m is the year, month, day, hour, minute and second, in
     the UTC timezone, of when player program started.

     [1mdebuglog.frogue[0m
            This file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/debuglog.frogue[0m

            records the standard  output  (stdout)  from  the  current  [1mrogue[22m(6)
            process.  When [1mrogomatic[22m(6) starts, this file is truncated to a zero
            size.

            The  player  process when starting, will create this file if it does
            not exist, or truncate it to zero size otherwise.  Thus,  this  file
            only holds data about the most recent run.

     [1mdebuglog.player[0m
            When [1mrogomatic[22m(6) launches player, this file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/debuglog.player[0m

            records tokens (output) written to the current [1mrogue[22m(6) process.

            The  player  program when starting, will create this file if it does
            not exist, or truncate it to zero size otherwise.  Thus,  this  file
            only holds data about the most recent run.

     [1merrlog[0m
            Output written to standard error (stderr) is appended to:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/errlog[0m

            If this file does not exist, it is created, otherwise it is appended
            to  by  the  player program.  Thus, this file may contain data about
            previous runs.

     [1merror5.4.5[0m
            A log of internal errors and bugs is kept in:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/error[4m[22mversion[0m

            where [4mversion[24m is the [1mrogue [22mversion number, such as [4m5.4.5[24m.

            If this file does not exist, it is created, otherwise it is appended
            to by the player program.  Thus, this file may  contain  data  about
            previous runs.

     [1mgamelog[0m
            This file is the rogomatic game log file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/gamelog[0m

            Lines  that  begin with [4mC:[24m record commands sent to [1mrogue[22m(6) by [1mrogo-[0m
            [1mmatic[22m(6).  Lines that begin with [4mR:[24m record the responses  sent  from
            [1mrogue[22m(6) to [1mrogomatic[22m(6).

            By using the [1m-p [22mcommand line option, one may replay such a rogomatic
            game  log.  When combined with a final [4mr_file[24m command line argument,
            the replay file will be [4mr_file[24m.

            When a "[1mgood game[22m" exits, the rogomatic game log  is  renamed  to  a
            file of the form:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/goodgame.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS.max_level.score.mnst[0m

            See  [1m-G [4m[22mgoodlvl[24m  in the [1mOPTIONS [22msection for information on what con-
            stitutes a "[1mgood game[22m".

            The player program when starting, will create this file if  it  does
            not  exist,  or  truncate  it to zero size otherwise.  Thus, if this
            file exists, only holds data about the most recent run  if  the  run
            wasn't a "[1mgood game[22m".

     [1mGeneLock545[0m
            Before [1mrogomatic[22m(6) and player writes a gene pool related file, this
            file is locked:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/GeneLoc[4m[22mversion[0m

            where  [4mversion[24m  is  the  [1mrogue [22mversion number, without dots, such as
            [4m545[24m.  This file is locked via the [1mflock[22m(2) system call.

            The player program when starting, will create this file if  it  does
            not exist.  This is a zero length file.

     [1mGeneLog545[0m
            A log of all mutations and crossovers is kept in:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/GeneLog[4m[22mversion[0m

            where  [4mversion[24m  is  the  [1mrogue [22mversion number, without dots, such as
            [4m545[24m.

            If this file does not exist, it is created, otherwise it is appended
            to by the player program.  Thus, this file may  contain  data  about
            previous runs.

     [1mGenePool545[0m
            The gene pool is kept in:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/GenePool[4m[22mversion[0m

            where  [4mversion[24m  is  the  [1mrogue [22mversion number, without dots, such as
            [4m545[24m.

            If this file does not exist, it is created, otherwise it is appended
            to by the player program.  Thus, this file may  contain  data  about
            previous runs.

     [1mgoodgame.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS.max_level.score.mnst[0m
            When  [1mrogomatic[22m(6) co then the [1mgamelog [22mwill be renamed under the ro-
            gomatic directory as

                 [1m@RGMDIR@[4m[22m/goodgame.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS.max_level.score.mnst[0m

            where [4mYYYYMMDD_HHMMSS[24m is the year, month, day, hour, minute and sec-
            ond, in the UTC timezone, and [4mmax_level[24m is the maximum dungeon level
            that was reached, and [4mscore[24m is the final rogue score,  and  [4mmnst[24m  is
            the first 4 characters of how the rogue died, or "[4mtota[24m" if the rouge
            was a total winner.

            This  file  is only created, by renaming [1mgamelog[22m, when a "[1mgood game[22m"
            ends.  See [1m-G [4m[22mgoodlvl[24m in the [1mOPTIONS [22msection for information on what
            constitutes a "[1mgood game[22m".


     [1mltm545[0m
            The long term memory of monster attributes is kept in:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/ltm[4m[22mversion[0m

            where [4mversion[24m is the [1mrogue [22mversion number,  without  dots,  such  as
            [4m545[24m.

            If  this  file does not exist, it is created, otherwise it is update
            by the player process.  Thus, this file may contain data about  pre-
            vious runs.

     [1mlvllog[0m
            Each time player detects that a new level has been reached, and when
            player  exits  (due  to rogue death, player quit, or even total win-
            ner), this file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/lvllog[0m

            will be appended with a line containing the  following  space  sepa-
            rated fields:

                   1. timestamp
                        microseconds since the epoch

                   2. UTC date
                        YYYY-MM-DD format

                   3. UTC time
                        time of day in HH:MM:DD format

                   4. HEADER: Level:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   5. level number
                        dungeon level number

                   6. HEADER: Gold:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   7. gold collected
                        amount of Gold collected so far

                   8. HEADER: Hp:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   9. hit points
                        current hit points followed by ( maximum hit points )

                   10. HEADER: Str:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   11. strength
                        current strength followed by ( maximum strength )

                   12. HEADER: Ac:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   13. armor class
                        current armor class

                   14. HEADER: Exp:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   15. experience
                        experience level followed by / experience points

                   16. HEADER: Am:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   17. amulet status
                        - ==> amulet not found , ==> [4mAmulet[24m [4mof[24m [4mYendor[24m found

                   18. end status

            The  player program, when starting, will create this file if it does
            not exist, or truncate it to zero size otherwise.  Thus,  this  file
            only holds data about the most recent run.

     [1mpidlog[0m
            Early on in player, this file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/pidlog[0m

            is  appended  with  a  line containing the following space separated
            fields:

                   1. timestamp
                        microseconds since the epoch

                   2. UTC date
                        YYYY-MM-DD format

                   3. UTC time
                        time of day in HH:MM:DD format

                   4. rogue-pid:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   5. rogue PID
                        rogue game process ID

                   6. HEADER: player-pid:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   7. player PID
                        player executable process ID

                   8. HEADER: dungeon:
                        a string ending in a colon

                   9. dnum
                        dungeon number

            If this file does not exist, it is created, otherwise it is appended
            to by the player program.  Thus, this file may  contain  data  about
            previous runs.

     [1mplayer.lck[0m
            When [1mrogomatic[22m(6) launches, this file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/player.lck[0m

            is  locked via [1mflock[22m(2) and then it released.  If the file cannot be
            locked, then [1mrogomatic[22m(6) will exit.  The purpose  of  this  initial
            temporary  lock is an attempt to detect if a player program has com-
            pleted its initial setup and is running.

            When [1mrogomatic[22m(6) executes the player program, early on this file is
            locked via [1mflock[22m(2) and is held until the program exits.  This  lock
            helps  prevent  more than one [1mrogomatic[22m(6) from using the same rogo-
            matic directory.

            The player program, when starting, will create this file if it  does
            not exist.  This is a zero length file.

     [1mRgm.Lock[0m
            Before  [1mrogomatic[22m(6)  writes  to  a  critical  file (e.g., [4mltm545[24m or
            [4mrgmdelta5.4.5[24m ) this file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/rgmdelta5.4.5[0m

            is locked via the [1mflock[22m(2) and then is released after the  write  is
            complete.

            The  player program, when starting, will create this file if it does
            not exist.  This is a zero length file.

     [1mrgmdelta5.4.5[0m
            The rogomatic delta file is:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/rgmdelta[4m[22mversion[0m

            where [4mversion[24m is the [1mrogue [22mversion number, such as [4m5.4.5[24m.

            When the [1m-s [22mcommand line option  is  used  to  print  the  rogomatic
            scoreboard,  the  rogomatic  delta file is merged into the rogomatic
            score file (the rogomatic score file is created if needed), and then
            the rogomatic delta file is removed before printing the result.

            If this file does not exist, it is created, otherwise it is appended
            to by the player process.  Thus, this file may  contain  data  about
            previous runs.

     [1mrgmscore5.4.5[0m
            The rogomatic score file is kept in:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/rgmscore[4m[22mversion[0m

            where [4mversion[24m is the [1mrogue [22mversion number, such as [4m5.4.5[24m.

            If  this file does not exist, it is created when the [1m-s [22mcommand line
            option is used.  Thus, this file may  contain  data  about  previous
            runs.

     [1mrogue.lck[0m
            A lock file used by [1mrogue[22m(6) to manage writing to its local files.

            When  [1mrogomatic[22m(6)  launches [1mrogue[22m(6), it does so in such a way that
            the program's default path is changed to:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/rogue.lck[0m

            If this file does not exist, it is created by [1mrogue[22m(6).  This  is  a
            zero length file.

     [1mrogue.scr[0m
            A score file containing the top scores of [1mrogue[22m(6).

            When  [1mrogomatic[22m(6)  launches [1mrogue[22m(6), it does so in such a way that
            the program's default path is changed to:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/rogue.sav[0m

            To view the rogue score file:

                 [1mrogue -s @RGMDIR@/rogue.lck[0m

            If this file does not exist, it is created by [1mrogue[22m(6).

     [1msaved_termattr[0m
            Before [1mrogomatic[22m(6) prepares the screen for [1mncurses[22m(3X) managed  I/O
            (such  as  raw  terminal mode), it saves the terminal state into the
            file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/saved_termattr[0m

            On exit, [1mrogomatic[22m(6) will use the contents of this file to the pre-
            vious (hopefully) sane terminal state.

            While running [1mrogue[22m(6) will create this file, and as it is  exiting,
            the file will be removed.

     [1mtotal.lvllog[0m
            Each time player detects that a new level has been reached, and when
            player  exits  (due  to rogue death, player quit, or even total win-
            ner), this file:

                 [1m@RGMDIR@/total.lvllog[0m

            See [1mlvllog [22mabove for a description of the contents of this file.

            Unlike [1mlvllog[22m, this file isn't truncated at the start of each run.

            If this file does not exist, it is created, otherwise it is appended
            to by the player process.  Thus, this file may  contain  data  about
            previous runs.

[1mSEE ALSO[0m
     [1mflock[22m(2), [1mncurses[22m(3X), [1mrogue[22m(6)

[1mSOURCE CODE[0m
     The source code for [1mrogomatic [22mis available is available at:

          [4mhttps://github.com/lcn2/rogomatic[0m

     in the form of a GitHub repo.

     The  above  GitHub  repo  is  maintained by: [1mLandon Curt Noll [22m( GitHub user
     [1m@lcn2[22m: [4mhttps://github.com/lcn2[24m ) with help from the GitHub user @jenoola  (
     [4mhttps://github.com/jenoola[24m ).

     The  port  to ISO C 2017 with GNU extensions, the port to various platforms
     such as NetBSD, Linux, and Cygwin, and many rogue bug fixes applied by Lan-
     don Curt Noll user [1m@lcn2[22m, and the GitHub user [1m@jenoola[22m.

[1mBUGS[0m
     Itas easy to imagine that the original authors were more focused on  craft-
     ing a scholarly paper rather than on creating well written code.

     A  number  of  people  have attempted to improve this code.  Many, many bug
     fixes have been applied to this code.  [1mLandon Curt Noll [22malone has a  change
     log  of  mostly  bug fixes, that in mid 2026, exceeded 1500 lines of change
     description text!  Even so, a [1msignificant number of  bugs  [22mremain  in  this
     code.

     Bug fixes in the form of GitHub pull requests are [1mvery welcome [22mat:

          [4mhttps://github.com/lcn2/rogomatic/pulls[0m

     You  are  likely to notice that [1mrogomatic[22m(6) will occasionally hang.  Often
     the code will hang when the state machine, expect a response from  [1mrogue[22m(6)
     encounters  an  unexpected message (e.g., announcing a new level, reporting
     hunger, coming under attack, removal of a temporary impairment, etc.)

     Occasionally [1mrogomatic [22mwill dead-lock waiting for input from  [1mrogue[22m.   When
     this  happens,  try  to type [4mt[24m in an effort to toggle [4mtransparent[24m mode.  If
     that is successful, type [4mt[24m again in the hopes of resuming the run.

     Sometimes you will have to use transparent mode to get it down to the  next
     level before turning it loose again.

     In  some hanging cases, try interrupting (usually by typing control C), and
     then entering the [4mS[24m command to save the game.  Once  saved,  re-start  with
     "rogomatic -r" and then [1mhope [22mthat the restart will be OK.

     One work-a-round to hanging is to use the [1m-a [4m[22msecs[24m command line option, such
     as using: [1m-a [4m[22m4.5[24m on the command line.

     When  the [1m-a [4m[22msecs[24m command line option is used, then before [1mrogomatic[22m(6) at-
     tempts to read from [1mrogue[22m(6), a timeout alarm will be  set.   If  the  read
     hangs for more than [4msecs[24m seconds, a [1mSIGARLM [22msignal will be delivered to the
     player process.  The [1mSIGARLM [22mis then caught and a [1msiglongjmp[22m(3) performed a
     non-local  jump to a control point where player process is much more likely
     to resume normal operation.  Nevertheless, there  are  circumstances  where
     non-local jump still hangs.

     While  less  often, [1mrogomatic[22m(6) will enter a loop, such as cycling between
     two positions, or cycling between pick up and dropping an item.  The  above
     mentioned  use  of keyboard intervention may help, if you catch the loop in
     time.  Without some form of intervention, the rogue will eventually die  of
     starvation.   As  with  real  life,  death  tends  to interrupt the flow of
     things.  :-)

[1mDEBUGGING[0m
     See the [1mBUGS [22msection above for why you are welcome to debug [1mrogomatic[22m(6).

     See also any open GitHub issues by visiting:

          [4mhttps://github.com/lcn2/rogomatic/issues[0m

     If you wish to debug [1mrogomatic[22m(6) we recommend compiling with the  [1mclang[22m(1)
     compiler, and debugging with the [1mlldb[22m(1) debugger as these tools do well to
     handle  to debugging across [1mfork (2) [22msystem calls, and cross process execu-
     tions such as making [1mexecl (2) [22msystem calls.

     You should first recompile [1mrogomatic[22m(6) with full debugging and C tags:

          [1mmake clobber clang tags[0m

     When debugging, you should use the [1m-d [22mcommand line option to always  create
     a  new rogomatic sub-directory so that the rogomatic the environment starts
     out in an initial state.  Attempting to debug an observed problem in a pre-
     vious run set (such as what will happen when using the [1mrerun_rogo  [22mcommand)
     will  be  problematic  because  [1mrogomatic[22m(6) is designed to evolve its game
     play.  By using the [1m-d [22mcommand line option an observed  behaviour  is  more
     likely to be reproducible, and a more likely to be successfully debugged.

     Using  the  [1m-d  [22mcommand line option will cause an accumulation of rogomatic
     sub-directories, so you might wish to clean out old rogomatic  sub-directo-
     ries from time to time by doing:

          [1mrm -rf /var/tmp/rogomatic/2*[0m

     Using  the [1m-S [22mseed command line option will cause the [1mrandom[22m(3) pseudo-ran-
     dom number generators to be seeded with a  fixed  value  via  a  [1msrandom[22m(3)
     call.   By default, both [1mrogomatic[22m(6) and [1mrogue[22m(6) with a seed that changes
     on each new invocation.  By using the [1m-S [22mseed command line option you force
     both programs to seed with the same value each time.  This will make behav-
     iour is more likely to be reproducible, and a more likely  to  be  success-
     fully debugged.

     By  using  the [1m-H [22mand the [1m-U [4m[22m0[24m command line option, you won't slow down the
     rogomatic operations, allowing  one  to  debug  deeper  dungeon  conditions
     sooner.

     The [4mseed[24m value can be any integer between 0 and 2147483647.  For example:

          [1m./rogomatic -H -U 0 -d -S 1989014291[0m

     Because  [1mrogomatic[22m(6)  makes extensive use of ncurses, and because puts the
     terminal into raw mode, simply running the command from within the debugger
     will very problematic.  Both [1mrogomatic[22m(6) and your debugger will fight  for
     control of the terminal, and you will [1mNOT [22mbe the winner.  Moreover, the use
     of  ncurses  by dueling programs will produce a mess on the screen.  Fortu-
     nately [1mlldb[22m(1) offers a clean way around this problem.

     Start the debugger on [1mrogomatic[22m(6) with both the [1m-d [22mand the [1m-S [22mseed command
     line option.  For example:

          [1mlldb -- ./rogomatic -H -U 0 -d -S 3[0m

     Once the debugging has started, set a breakpoint somewhere with the  player
     process:

          [1mb getrogue[0m

     Don't  worry  about  the  debugger complaining about not being able resolve
     breakpoint to any actual locations.  That will resolve itself later on.

     Next, launch the process in a separate terminal window.   This  will  cause
     another terminal window to pop-up:

          [1mprocess launch --tty[0m

     [1mNOTE[22m:  Depending  on your terminal manager, keyboard focus may shift to the
     new terminal window pop-up.  You may have the click back on original termi-
     nal window so that your keyboard actions don't get consumed by the new ter-
     minal window.

     Have the debugger continue the process:

          [1mc[0m

     Best wishes in debugging.  If you find a bug fix, please  consider  submit-
     ting a GitHub pull requests are [1mvery welcome [22mat:

          [4mhttps://github.com/lcn2/rogomatic/pulls[0m


